# No loaded guns: never commit weights, models, or harmful/harmless prompt sets.
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.gguf
*.pt
*.pth
models/
abliterated/
out/
checkpoints/
results/
plots/

# THE ONE EXCEPTION TO `results/`, and it is deliberately narrow.
#
# `CONTRACT.md` section 4 requires a record of every head-to-head run, and for a month there was
# none for the 2026-09-10 one: the figures on the public benchmark page existed on a single laptop
# and nowhere a reader could reach. The review panel could not check them, and the first correction
# to that page consequently carried two numbers taken from a synthetic fixture rather than from the
# run. A published measurement whose artefacts are unreachable is a measurement nobody can dispute,
# which is not a virtue.
#
# So aggregate JSON under `head-to-head/results/` is re-admitted, and NOTHING else is. The heading
# above still governs: `*.safetensors`, `*.pt`, `*.pth`, `*.bin` and `*.gguf` are matched by their
# own patterns and stay excluded wherever they sit, and `.jsonl` is deliberately NOT re-admitted
# because that is the extension the per-prompt margin rows use, and those carry prompt text and the
# replies to it. `tests/test_published_head_to_head.py` asserts both halves of that, so the
# exception cannot quietly widen.
!head-to-head/results/
!head-to-head/results/*/
!head-to-head/results/**/*.json
!head-to-head/results/**/*.md
# datasets (the harmful/harmless contrast + eval sets stay private)
ds/
*_ds/
data/
*.arrow
# THE PACKAGE'S OWN data directory was collateral damage from the line above. `data/` has no
# leading slash, so git matches it at any depth, and `src/senbonzakura/data/` was therefore in
# nobody's clone. That is not a tidiness problem: it made `--chat-template plain` fail on a
# fresh clone naming a template the tool claims to ship, and it is why `doctor` reported
# corpora "that have never existed" on a second machine. Found on 2026-09-08 by a peer running
# a pinned clone; a tar of the working tree would never have shown it.
#
# The templates come back in. They are Jinja chat formats with no prompts in them. Git cannot
# un-ignore a file inside an ignored DIRECTORY, so the parent has to be re-included first and
# then narrowed again; that is why this is four lines rather than one negation.
!src/senbonzakura/data/
src/senbonzakura/data/*
!src/senbonzakura/data/templates/
!src/senbonzakura/data/templates/*.jinja
# The capability probe is committed rather than generated, for the opposite reason to the blobs
# below: it is 256 GSM8K arithmetic questions under MIT, with no prompts and nothing gated. The
# blobs are generated precisely BECAUSE they are harmful, and applying that rule to harmless
# content would leave a clone install unable to run the capability gate that is now on by default,
# which is the 2026-09-22 `--track default` defect on the one gate whose absence is hardest to
# notice. Its negation cannot live here, because `*.jsonl` further down would undo it; see there.
# `corpora.bin` and `default-track.bin` DELIBERATELY STAY OUT, and the rule above keeps them
# out. They hold 4,895 harmful prompts under a shipping key, and the project publishes that
# corpus as a GATED dataset on purpose. The wheel carries them because building one is a
# deliberate act that attaches the licence notice; a public git tree would hand them to a
# crawler and remove the gate. A clone rebuilds them, and the tool says how when they are
# absent rather than failing obscurely.
# Retained generations and per-prompt margins. These hold harmful prompts and the
# replies a model gave to them, so the default is out.
*.jsonl
# The capability probe is the other explicit exception, and it has to sit AFTER `*.jsonl` rather
# than beside the other data/ negations: git applies the last matching rule, so a negation written
# earlier is silently undone here. `git check-ignore -v` is the only way to know, and it caught
# exactly that on 2026-09-22. The file holds 256 GSM8K arithmetic questions under MIT: no prompts,
# no generations, nothing the rule above exists to keep out.
!src/senbonzakura/data/capability-gsm8k.jsonl
# evidence/ is the one committed home, and it needs an explicit negation: the rule
# above would otherwise force `git add -f`, which defeats the ignore it bypasses.
# Files landing here must already have their prompt and generation fields stripped,
# which tools/ci/check_prompt_artefacts.py enforces rather than trusts.
!evidence/
!evidence/**/*.jsonl
# python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
build/
dist/
# Every scratch build directory, not each one as it is invented. `dist-adv/` was not
# listed and a 19 MB wheel reached a commit in a repository with a public remote.
dist-*/
# The clean room's last-resort work directory, used only when /tmp and /var/tmp are both too
# small for a full install. It holds a multi-gigabyte venv, so it is ignored for the same
# reason `dist-*/` is: a scratch directory inside a repository with a public remote.
.cleanroom-work/
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.optuna*/
optuna*.db

# The committed toy track. Every dataset rule above catches a save_to_disk layout, so
# the one example a stranger can run has to re-include the DIRECTORY before its
# contents: git does not descend into an excluded directory, so negating only the files
# would do nothing. Its prompts are synthetic placeholders, never real harmful text.
!examples/toy-track/
!examples/toy-track/**/
!examples/toy-track/**

# Terminal captures used only to verify a recording caught what it claims.
# Not published, and the banner it captures is randomised, so it is not a golden file.
docs/tapes/captured/

# Vendored third-party artefacts: FETCHED at build time, never committed.
#
# `src/senbonzakura/vendor/pins.json` IS committed and is the whole record: it names the upstream,
# the tag, the publication date, the expected sizes and the sha256 of everything fetched. So what a
# wheel contains is reviewable in a diff of that one file rather than by unzipping a wheel.
#
# What is deliberately NOT here:
#   * bin/ holds llama-quantize and its shared libraries, 27 MB for one platform and roughly 80 MB
#     for the six. Committing opaque blobs of that size to a public repository, per pin bump, buys
#     nothing a hash does not already give.
#   * src/ holds llama.cpp's conversion package: 158 modules, about 752 KB. It was going to be
#     committed on the argument that source is reviewable in a diff, and that argument does not
#     survive contact with the actual number. A 158-file diff on every pin bump is the appearance
#     of reviewability rather than the fact of it.
#
# `python tools/packaging/vendor_llama.py --all` repopulates both, verifies every hash against pins.json,
# and runs the binary to prove the extraction produced something that starts.
src/senbonzakura/vendor/bin/
src/senbonzakura/vendor/src/

# Fetched corpus sources. `src/senbonzakura/vendor/corpora-pins.json` IS committed and records the
# upstream, the pinned commit, the byte count and the sha256 of every file, so what the wheel
# carries is reviewable in a diff of one small file. The CSVs themselves are other people's data
# at a pinned commit; re-fetch with `python tools/packaging/build_corpora.py`.
build/corpora/
